When a demo expands an input into a larger rectangle, do not jump directly to local recoloring or object construction. Derive how each output row and column maps to source coordinates from the complete demo. Test hypotheses such as repeated rows, mirrored sequences, or a periodic traversal with duplicated turning points. Verify every output cell, especially the boundary and turning indices.

Apply row and column generators independently to the test's dimensions; do not assume the result is square just because a demo happened to be square. Validate the resulting dimensions and exact source-cell mapping before submitting. This is a hypothesis-generation checklist, not a claim that every expansion uses reflection.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u025/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-budgeted-inference/references/expansion-axis-inference.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint-update", "file": "references/sparse-line-gap-checkpoint.md", "content": "# Sparse collinear gap inference

When demos contain separated runs or sparse markers of one color, compare exact changes along both axes. Record maximal same-color runs and isolated markers by row/column, then test whether output fills only cells between collinear same-color endpoints. Determine endpoint eligibility and scope independently: identical-looking gaps may remain unchanged in an identity or partial-transformation demo. A broad “fill every gap” rule is not supported unless it reproduces every changed and unchanged cell across the cumulative demo ledger.

After any rejected prediction, discard the complete fill generator and revisit trigger, orientation, and target selection; do not merely remove a few gaps. This is a candidate mechanism to test against the full examples, not a universal ARC rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u025/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint-update/references/sparse-line-gap-checkpoint.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/trajectory-after-error.md", "content": "# Revising sparse trajectory hypotheses after a wrong output

A wrong submission that extends only one apparent ray is not repaired by shifting its start or changing a single cell. Re-derive the generator from each demo by recording every seed marker, color, direction vector, and endpoint. In the example reviewed here, one demo was identity (a sparse object did not trigger extension), while the larger demo showed simultaneous rays: a 7-ray extended down-right from its marker, and 6-ray(s) extended along the other diagonal direction from multiple marker locations. The test had several 9 markers plus paired 7 and 6 markers; assuming the salient 9-marker cluster was irrelevant and extending just one 6 ray / one short 7 ray was unsupported and was rejected. General lesson: compare the full input geometry to positive and negative demos; infer whether multiple marker colors or every compatible seed launches a ray, and project each ray through the full grid to the evidenced boundary. Preserve original objects and background. Do not submit a one-color or one-ray interpretation unless it accounts for all trajectory behavior in the demonstrations.

This example is not a universal rule for launching rays from all markers: use it to formulate competing hypotheses, then select the one that explains the complete demos and test configuration.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u025/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-error-recovery/references/trajectory-after-error.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-mixed-demo-review", "file": "references/cellwise-mask-and-generator-validation.md", "content": "# Cellwise mask validation across heterogeneous demos

When an ARC task has a distinctive colored seed in noisy grids, do not equate resemblance with the rule. Track the seed shape, eligible target values, and spatial generator as independent hypotheses. For every demo, enumerate the full input/output difference mask and preserve negative evidence: unchanged lookalikes, untouched target-valued cells, and the original seed.

Before another demo or a submission, replay the proposed generator against every pair, especially when seed shape, palette, or dimensions vary. A literal translated-template matcher that looks plausible on one example but fails the varied demos is falsified; do not patch it by shifting a few cells. Request additional examples only for explicit remaining alternatives and a specific discriminating observation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u025/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-mixed-demo-review/references/cellwise-mask-and-generator-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-evidence-workflow", "file": "references/session-lesson.md", "content": "# Session lesson: motif resemblance is not a generator

In a Continual-ARC stream, a series of heterogeneous examples can tempt the solver to label each task as colored-template copying based on a few salient placements. One predicted mask can even look plausible on the test while failing because its translation/target assumptions were not checked against the complete examples. The reliable method is to extract the exact seed and complete diff for each pair, separate target eligibility from shape and placement, and enumerate candidates against all changed and unchanged cells. Integrate each new pair immediately; do not spend most of the demo budget on reflexive requests. After wrong feedback, discard the whole rule and make a materially revised inference rather than repeating nearby guesses. Keep every puzzle turn to exactly one action JSON object.

Tool-use lesson: empty/no-op calls and retries against unavailable reference filenames produced no evidence. Use the concrete coordinate computation path and check the available-files listing for the owning skill before requesting a specific reference.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u025/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-evidence-workflow/references/session-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/sparse-geometry-vs-template.md", "content": "# Sparse geometry versus motif copying

When a sparse grid contains a compact colored motif plus isolated same-color marks, the motif may cue a geometric operation rather than serve as a template to replicate. Record the exact motif anchor and every marker coordinate. Test whether aligned markers define endpoints/borders and whether intervening cells are completed; infer eligible orientation, endpoint class, and scope separately. Account for existing markers that remain unchanged. Compare this with literal template-occurrence predictions against every demo, including negative evidence, before acting. A varied second geometry can discriminate the candidate mechanisms; request it only when it could alter the prediction.
