{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-budgeted-inference", "file": "references/failed-demo-chain-review.md", "content": "# Failed demo-chain review: integrate before repeating a request

In this session, four heterogeneous demo pairs were returned, but each response was followed by another `request_demos` action without a meaningful update to the hypothesis set. The fifth demo might still be useful, but only after the accumulated evidence is actually analyzed. Earlier examples include a single-cell recoloring, a shape recoloring, a shape retained unchanged, and a tiny pair of cells whose color changes; the fourth pair similarly appears to recolor a localized 9-shaped cluster to 7 while preserving other components. These diverse local diffs do not establish a common motif-copy or ray transformation.

Reusable procedure: after each pair, compare input/output cellwise and record changed coordinates and exact old/new values, including whether the pair is an identity. Compare the local transformations across pairs for relational trigger, scope, or shared structure; keep unrelated-looking examples in the ledger rather than discarding them. Do not send a demo request as an automatic continuation. Request another only after naming live alternatives and a concrete observation that would discriminate them. The lesson is process-level; it does not assert a solved generator for this instance.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u025/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-budgeted-inference/references/failed-demo-chain-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-mixed-demo-review", "file": "references/seed-motif-generator-checkpoint.md", "content": "# Seed-motif generator checkpoint after a wrong submission

A salient colored seed embedded in a noisy grid does not by itself imply a copy rule. Candidate classes can include translated shape occurrences, periodic/lattice replication, directional rays, or combinations. Before extrapolating, record the seed's exact coordinates and the complete input/output difference mask in every demo. Verify both positive and negative cases: why each changed cell is selected, and why visually similar unchanged cells are excluded. Check palettes and dimensions that vary across demos; distinguish the seed color, target/background color, and unrelated noise empirically rather than assuming one role from a single pair.

After an incorrect submission, discard the whole generator. Do not move a few cells, swap a presumed background color, or switch to a nearby geometric interpretation without replaying it against every demo. With a costly attempt budget, use remaining demos for explicit competing hypotheses and only submit after a full-mask validation. Preserve all cells outside the supported transformation and verify dimensions/row lengths.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u025/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-mixed-demo-review/references/seed-motif-generator-checkpoint.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/multi-rejection-demo-discipline.md", "content": "# Multi-rejection discipline for demo-driven ARC

In a task with one or more incorrect submissions, an incorrect result falsifies the whole proposed generator. Do not resubmit a near-identical grid or make an evidence-free palette/coordinate tweak. Rebuild a ledger from every input-output pair, including unchanged cells, and separately test scope, geometry, mapping, and palette.

When multiple candidate grids have failed and the remaining evidence does not determine the test output, stop guessing. State internally at least two concrete hypotheses and the specific observation that distinguishes them. Request one demo only if it can supply that observation, then integrate it before the next action; never treat receiving a demo as an automatic reason for another request. Submit only when one candidate explains every pair and unambiguously yields the test grid. Keep each turn to one action-only JSON object.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u025/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-error-recovery/references/multi-rejection-demo-discipline.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/separator-vs-motif-check.md", "content": "# Full-width separator versus salient interior motif

When a test grid has a complete horizontal/vertical line alongside a conspicuous patterned region, don't select a transform just because the motif is visually rich. Start from exact input/output diffs in every demo. Record separator coordinates and whether separator cells change, plus every changed isolated marker and unchanged lookalikes. Compare candidate rules that operate on the separator/bands against rules operating on the motif, and require a single candidate to explain all positive and negative cells before projecting to the test.

In this instance, a minimal demo changed a sparse marker and recolored the adjacent full-width row below a full-width divider; another recolored the row immediately above a full-width divider while preserving a lone marker. The test had a bottom full-width color-2 row and an unrelated dense color-1 patch. The initial guess recolored the row above the divider and copied the patch as if it were a template; it was rejected. Do not infer a color-8 band merely by mirroring the first demo's local row offset. Need more discriminating evidence to establish which band relative to divider is selected and what sparse markers indicate.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u025/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint/references/separator-vs-motif-check.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/session-consecutive-demo-synthesis.md", "content": "# Consecutive demo requests without synthesis

In this instance, five demo requests were made in succession across turns, despite each new pair changing the evidence materially. The examples looked unlike each other locally, but their salient relation was a long straight divider/marker line in color 2 plus an object on one side. In demonstrations, the output added a parallel line of color 8 on the opposite side of the object/partition, preserving the original line and object. The test similarly has a vertical 2 line at column 14 and a compact 0 motif immediately to its left; this suggests completing a parallel 8 line at column 11. This is the kind of structural synthesis that should happen after each pair, not after exhausting several demos.

Operational lesson: after every returned demo, record divider orientation/location, side containing the object, output line orientation/location, and preserved cells. Compare candidate relations across all prior examples. Do not issue the next request unless a specific unresolved fork remains and a new pair could distinguish it. A repeated pattern of asking for demos without stating the evolving hypotheses spends budget without increasing useful evidence.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u025/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint/references/session-consecutive-demo-synthesis.md"}

